   <text:p text:style-name="Cuerpo_20_de_20_texto.cap">Figure 1.1 — choz just opened: the menu bar, an empty RACK, the IN and OUT drawers at the edges, and the MONITOR panel along the bottom.</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2383_3455341890"/>1.1 What choz is<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2383_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">choz is an audio plugin host for the terminal. It gives you a rack of tabs; each tab takes notes or audio in, loads an instrument, chains effects onto it, and leaves either through the main mix or through a port of its own on the JACK/PipeWire graph.</text:p>
   <text:list text:style-name="L1">
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P10"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Instruments:</text:span> SoundFont (SF2), SFZ, WAV, and CLAP, LV2, VST2, VST3, DSSI and LADSPA plugins, plus Pure Data patches. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P10"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Effects:</text:span> 56 of choz&apos;s own, plus every effect plugin installed on the machine, up to twelve per tab. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P10"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Note sources:</text:span> any MIDI port, choz&apos;s own ALSA and JACK ports, OSC, the computer keyboard, an arpeggiator and a step sequencer per tab, and audio-to-note tracking from a microphone. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P11"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Outputs:</text:span> the main stereo mix, four groups, and one direct out per tab for recording each tab on its own track in a DAW. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
   </text:list>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2385_3455341890"/>1.2 About this guide<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2385_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">Chapters 1 to 3 are the introduction: what choz needs, how to install it, and what is on the screen. Chapter 2 is a short walk-through that gets a sound out of it. Chapters 4 to 13 are the reference, one chapter per area of the program. Chapters 14 and 15 are lookup tables — shortcuts, environment variables, files. Chapter 16 collects the problems people actually hit, and chapter 17 is a glossary of the terms used throughout.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2387_3455341890"/>1.3 Document conventions<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2387_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <table:table table:name="Tabla2" table:style-name="Tabla2">
    <table:table-column table:style-name="Tabla2.A"/>
    <table:table-column table:style-name="Tabla2.B"/>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla2.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Heading">Convention</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla2.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Heading">Meaning</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla2.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="P12">BOLD CAPITALS</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla2.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Something written on the screen: a button, a panel, a knob — <text:span text:style-name="T2">MIXER</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">PANIC</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">SENS</text:span>.</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla2.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents"><text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Monospace</text:span></text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla2.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Something you type, or a name outside choz: a command, a file, a port — <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">choz:midi_in</text:span>.</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla2.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">F2, Enter, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">a</text:span></text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla2.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">A key to press. Capitals mean the shifted key: <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">A</text:span> is Shift+a.</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla2.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">→</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla2.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">A path through menus: EDIT → Settings → THEME.</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
   </table:table>
   <table:table table:name="Tabla3" table:style-name="Tabla3">
    <table:table-column table:style-name="Tabla3.A"/>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla3.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Note.</text:span> Grey boxes like this one carry something worth knowing before it bites: a limit, an interaction with another program, or a setting that only takes effect on the next start.</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
   </table:table>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2389_3455341890"/>1.4 System requirements<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2389_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <table:table table:name="Tabla4" table:style-name="Tabla4">
    <table:table-column table:style-name="Tabla4.A"/>
    <table:table-column table:style-name="Tabla4.B"/>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla4.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Heading">Requirement</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla4.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Heading">Detail</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla4.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">System</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla4.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Linux with glibc; x86-64, aarch64 or armv7</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla4.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Audio</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla4.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents"><text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">libasound.so.2</text:span> (required); <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">libjack.so.0</text:span> (optional, opened at runtime — normally PipeWire&apos;s, from <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">pipewire-jack</text:span>)</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla4.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Terminal</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla4.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">24-bit colour, at least 80×24 — 120×40 to work comfortably. kitty, Ghostty and WezTerm also draw the wallpaper through the terminal&apos;s graphics protocol</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla4.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Plugin windows</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla4.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">X11 or XWayland, and only to open a plugin&apos;s own window: every plugin parameter also appears as a knob in the rack</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla4.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Privileges</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla4.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents"><text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">rtprio</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">memlock</text:span> (membership of the <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">audio</text:span> group) recommended for small buffers</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
   </table:table>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2391_3455341890"/>1.5 Installing<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2391_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text"># from a release (no toolchain needed)</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">tar xzf choz-1.3.18-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">cd choz-1.3.18-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">./install.sh</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text"># from the repository</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">cargo build --release</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13">./packaging/install.sh --binary target/release/choz</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">There are also <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">.deb</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">.rpm</text:span> packages, and an Arch <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">PKGBUILD</text:span>. All of them add a desktop entry under Audio, so choz can be started from the application menu as well as from a shell.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">The install leaves four things besides the binary: <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">choz.clap</text:span> (choz&apos;s 56 effects plus the arpeggiator, the step sequencer, the metronome and the arranger, as CLAP plugins for other hosts), <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">choz-rack.clap</text:span> (choz itself as a CLAP instrument, section 2.5), the wallpapers, and — when libpd is present — the <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">choz-pd-host</text:span> binary that runs Pure Data patches.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Upgrading.</text:span> <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">install.sh</text:span> lists what it is about to replace — the binaries with their <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">--version</text:span>, and <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">~/.clap/choz.clap</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">~/.clap/choz-rack.clap</text:span> — and asks before it does. <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">--yes</text:span> skips the question; run without a terminal (a script, CI) it replaces, as it always has.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Packages and old copies in ~/.clap.</text:span> A CLAP host reads <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">~/.clap</text:span> before <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">/usr/lib/clap</text:span>, so a bundle left there by an earlier <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">install.sh</text:span> hides the one a package installs and the host keeps running the old version. The <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">.deb</text:span> asks (debconf) whether to set those copies aside; the <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">.rpm</text:span> and the Arch package cannot ask and set them aside directly. Set aside means renamed to <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">*.bak</text:span>, which no host loads and which can be renamed back.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T2">JACK.</text:span> Current desktops run PipeWire with its JACK layer (<text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">pipewire-jack</text:span>), which is all choz needs for the graph; jack2 works the same. Neither is required: without libjack choz runs on ALSA.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2393_3455341890"/>1.6 What an uninstall leaves behind<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2393_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">No uninstaller touches <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">~/.local/state/choz</text:span>. Projects, plugin paths, scan caches and interface preferences live there and survive upgrades and removals; see section 15 for what each file holds.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">Everything else goes. Removing the <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">.deb</text:span>, the <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">.rpm</text:span> or the Arch package removes <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">/usr/lib/clap/choz.clap</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">/usr/lib/clap/choz-rack.clap</text:span> with the program; <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">install.sh --uninstall</text:span> removes the binaries, the desktop files and both bundles from <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">~/.clap</text:span>, and names each file as it goes.</text:p>
   <text:list text:style-name="L2">
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14">Left on purpose: <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">~/.local/state/choz</text:span> (projects and settings).</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14">Left because it is yours: any <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">*.clap.bak</text:span> a package set aside in <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">~/.clap</text:span>. Hosts do not load them; delete them by hand.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P15">Left by the host, not by choz: its plugin cache. Rescan plugins in the DAW so it forgets choz.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
   </text:list>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2395_3455341890"/>1.7 Starting choz<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2395_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">choz <text:s text:c="19"/># an empty rack</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">choz project.yml <text:s text:c="7"/># open a project</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">choz instrument.sf2 <text:s text:c="4"/># load a file straight into a tab</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">choz --osc-port 9000 <text:s text:c="3"/># pin the OSC port instead of letting the OS pick</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">choz --version <text:s text:c="9"/># print the version and exit</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13">choz --help <text:s text:c="12"/># print the options and exit</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2397_3455341890"/>1.8 Getting help<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2397_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">HELP → About choz shows the version and the build. The log at <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">~/.local/state/choz/choz.log</text:span> is the first place to look when something goes wrong, and chapter 16 explains the messages that are worth acting on. Running choz from a terminal also prints its diagnostics to standard error as they happen.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_2" text:outline-level="2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2399_3455341890"/>2. First steps<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2399_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">This chapter gets a sound out of choz and back into a DAW. It assumes nothing has been configured.</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2401_3455341890"/>2.1 Play a SoundFont from a MIDI keyboard<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2401_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:list text:style-name="L3">
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P16">Start choz. The rack is empty and says so. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P16">Press <text:span text:style-name="T2">F2</text:span> to open the <text:span text:style-name="T2">IN</text:span> drawer. The note inputs are at the top: every MIDI port on the system, choz&apos;s own ports, and OSC. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P16">Move to your keyboard&apos;s port and press <text:span text:style-name="T2">Enter</text:span>. choz makes tab 1 and binds the port to it — the row says → <text:span text:style-name="T2">tab 1</text:span>. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P16">Press <text:span text:style-name="T2">F2</text:span> again to close the drawer, then <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">1</text:span> to open the source picker. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P16">Type part of a SoundFont&apos;s name, or open the <text:span text:style-name="T2">SF2</text:span> tab, and press <text:span text:style-name="T2">Enter</text:span>. The bank loads into the tab. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P17">Play. If nothing sounds, check the <text:span text:style-name="T2">MONITOR</text:span> panel at the bottom: it shows every MIDI message as it arrives, so it answers &quot;is the keyboard reaching choz?&quot; before you look anywhere else. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
   </text:list>
