   <text:p text:style-name="Cuerpo_20_de_20_texto.cap">Figure 2.1 — Accepting the MIDI row: choz makes tab 1 and feeds it from that port.</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2403_3455341890"/>2.2 Add an effect<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2403_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:list text:style-name="L4">
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P18">With the tab active, press <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">a</text:span> to open the effect catalogue. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P18">Pick a category on the left — <text:span text:style-name="T2">REVERB</text:span>, say — and an effect on the right. <text:span text:style-name="T2">BUILT-IN</text:span> at the top narrows the list to choz&apos;s own. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P18">Press <text:span text:style-name="T2">Enter</text:span>. The effect is added to the tab&apos;s chain with all of its knobs. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P19">Move between knobs with ↑ ↓, change one with the wheel or <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">w</text:span>/<text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">s</text:span>, and switch the whole effect on or off with <text:span text:style-name="T2">Space</text:span>. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
   </text:list>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2405_3455341890"/>2.3 Play choz from a DAW and record it back<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2405_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">The usual round trip is: the DAW&apos;s MIDI out plays a choz tab, and choz&apos;s audio comes back into the DAW to be recorded.</text:p>
   <text:list text:style-name="L5">
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P20"><text:span text:style-name="T2">MIDI in.</text:span> In your DAW&apos;s MIDI device list, pick <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">choz MIDI IN</text:span> as the track&apos;s output. choz publishes that ALSA port itself, so nothing else is needed — see section 4.2. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P20"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Audio out.</text:span> In choz, open EDIT → Settings → <text:span text:style-name="T2">AUDIO</text:span> and set the backend to <text:span text:style-name="T2">JACK</text:span>, then restart choz. It now has <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">choz:out_1..N</text:span> on the graph. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P20"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Patch.</text:span> With any patchbay (qpwgraph, Carla, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">jack_connect</text:span>), wire <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">choz:out_1</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">choz:out_2</text:span> into the DAW&apos;s inputs, and arm a track on them. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Per-tab tracks.</text:span> To record several tabs separately, give each one its own direct out (section 4.6) and patch those instead of the main mix. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
   </text:list>
   <table:table table:name="Tabla5" table:style-name="Tabla5">
    <table:table-column table:style-name="Tabla5.A"/>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla5.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Note.</text:span> On PipeWire, its JACK layer is enough — there is no need to start a separate <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">jackd</text:span>. The MIDI half of this round trip does not need the graph at all: <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">choz MIDI IN</text:span> is an ALSA sequencer port.</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
   </table:table>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2407_3455341890"/>2.4 Save the project<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2407_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">Press <text:span text:style-name="T2">F10</text:span>, open <text:span text:style-name="T2">FILE</text:span> and choose <text:span text:style-name="T2">Save as</text:span>. A project is a readable YAML file that carries the rack, the routing, the effect chains, and the plugins&apos; own state — the patch you chose in a plugin&apos;s browser comes back with the project.</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___TocChoz1_2026"/>2.5 Run choz inside a DAW (REAPER)<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___TocChoz1_2026"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">Section 2.3 runs choz beside the DAW and wires the two together. The other way is to put choz <text:span text:style-name="T2">inside</text:span> it: <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">choz-rack.clap</text:span> is the whole rack as a CLAP instrument on one track — its own window, every tab, and the project saved in the host&apos;s session.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P22">REAPER</text:p>
   <text:list text:style-name="L2">
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14">Options → Preferences → Plug-ins → CLAP: make sure <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">/usr/lib/clap</text:span> (packages) or <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">~/.clap</text:span> (<text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">install.sh</text:span>) is on the path, and press <text:span text:style-name="T2">Re-scan</text:span>.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14">Insert <text:span text:style-name="T2">choz (choz)</text:span> as a virtual instrument on a track. Its window is choz&apos;s interface, keyboard and mouse included.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14">The track&apos;s MIDI reaches the rack as the host&apos;s note port: a tab set to listen to it (section 4.1) plays what the track sends.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14">The track&apos;s audio arrives as <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">host:in_1</text:span> / <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">host:in_2</text:span>, so a tab with that input processes it: the rack is an effect chain too.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Sixteen stereo outputs.</text:span> Put a tab on output pair 4 in the rack and it comes out of the plugin&apos;s fourth pair. In the FX chain, right-click the plugin → <text:span text:style-name="T2">Build multichannel routing for output of selected FX</text:span> to get a track per pair, the way a multi-out sampler is recorded.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P15">The rack is stored in the REAPER project; reopening the project reopens the rack.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
   </text:list>
   <table:table table:name="TablaChoz101" table:style-name="TablaChoz101">
    <table:table-column table:style-name="TablaChoz101.A"/>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="TablaChoz101.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Note.</text:span> The embedded rack keeps <text:span text:style-name="T2">its own tempo, time signature and play state</text:span>: it takes notes and MIDI from the host but not the host&apos;s transport. Set choz&apos;s tempo (TAP, or the metronome&apos;s menu) to the session&apos;s. The plugins in <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">choz.clap</text:span> — effects, arpeggiator, sequencer, metronome and arranger as separate plugins — do follow the host&apos;s transport. Only one rack runs per process.</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
   </table:table>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <table:table table:name="TablaChoz102" table:style-name="TablaChoz102">
    <table:table-column table:style-name="TablaChoz102.A"/>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="TablaChoz102.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents"><text:span text:style-name="T2">If REAPER shows an old version.</text:span> A copy of <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">choz-rack.clap</text:span> or <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">choz.clap</text:span> in <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">~/.clap</text:span> wins over <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">/usr/lib/clap</text:span>. Remove or rename it, quit REAPER completely (the old one is loaded in memory) and re-scan.</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
   </table:table>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_2" text:outline-level="2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2409_3455341890"/>3. choz overview<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2409_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2411_3455341890"/>3.1 The screen<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2411_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">The screen has four fixed areas: the menu bar along the top, the RACK in the middle, the IN and OUT drawers at the left and right edges, and the MONITOR / KEYS / WAVE / MIXER panel along the bottom. The last line is the status line.</text:p>
