   <text:p text:style-name="Cuerpo_20_de_20_texto.cap"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2435_3455341890"/>4.5 Audio inputs and the microphone<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2435_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">An audio input binds to a tab exactly like a note input: open IN, pick the channel, press Enter. The tab gains two controls of its own — <text:span text:style-name="T2">IN</text:span>, the input trim (<text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">&lt;</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">&gt;</text:span>), and <text:span text:style-name="T2">SENS</text:span>, the sensitivity of the audio-to-note follower (<text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">;</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">:</text:span>) — and the <text:span text:style-name="T2">A→M</text:span> button, which turns what comes in into MIDI notes for the instrument or for other tabs.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">FOLLOW sits beside WET while A→M is on. Off (the default) a note is a note, which is what a synth played from a guitar wants. On, the instrument also follows the input between notes: how far the note has fallen from its own peak goes out as CC 11 (expression; 40 dB under the peak is 0) and its drift from the note as pitch bend (±2 semitones), so a recorded sound breathes and bends with whatever drives it. Only what changes is sent, and switching it off puts expression back to full and the bend to the centre. It is saved with the project.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr1" draw:name="Imagen6" text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:width="15.499cm" svg:height="8.4cm"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png">
