   <text:p text:style-name="Cuerpo_20_de_20_texto.cap">Figure 4.3 — A stereo microphone bound to tab 3: the IN row with the trim, SENS, and the A→M button.</text:p>
   <text:list text:style-name="L7">
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P25">A mono channel stays mono all the way through — one fader on the mixer, one port on the graph — until something in the chain has a reason to pull the two sides apart. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P25"><text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">n</text:span> levels the tab against what it has played since it was loaded; <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">N</text:span> levels the whole rack. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P26">The feedback guard (section 13.1) cuts the loop when a microphone starts to howl. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P26"><text:span text:style-name="T2">choz as a microphone, and as an effect for the whole system.</text:span> Under the JACK/PipeWire backend choz adds two devices to the system while it runs, and takes them away when it closes. <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">choz Mic</text:span> is a microphone that carries the main mix — what is in the headphones — so Meet, Zoom, Teams or WhatsApp can pick it as their microphone. <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">choz System FX</text:span> is a speaker: set it as the system output and whatever the system plays arrives in the IN drawer as SYSTEM AUDIO — choz System FX, ready to be bound to a tab and given effects. Neither is offered as choz&apos;s own output (a speaker nobody hears), and an output saved as one of them is ignored. In a call, keep the real headphones as the system output: a call routed into choz System FX and back out through choz Mic is heard by the others as an echo. <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">CHOZ_NO_VIRTUAL_DEVICES=1</text:span> turns them off. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
   </text:list>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2437_3455341890"/>4.6 Direct outs<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2437_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">See section 11.3: a tab can leave the master mix through a pair of ports of its own, which is how a DAW records each tab on its own track.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_2" text:outline-level="2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2439_3455341890"/>5. Instruments<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2439_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2441_3455341890"/>5.1 The source picker<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2441_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">With a tab active, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">1</text:span> (or <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">i</text:span>) opens the picker. The tabs across the top filter by format — ALL, CLAP, SF2, WAV, SFZ, LV2, VST2, VST3, DSSI, LADSPA. Type to search, move with the arrows, accept with Enter; <text:span text:style-name="T2">F5</text:span> rescans.</text:p>
