   <text:p text:style-name="Cuerpo_20_de_20_texto.cap">Figure 5.6 — The sampler playing Philharmonia&apos;s cello: the note&apos;s own waveform with its start mark, the envelope, and the knobs.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T2">How a folder becomes an instrument.</text:span> File names first — <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">violin_C4_mf_RR2.wav</text:span> says the note, the dynamic and the take — then the folder names, then, only for what is still open, the audio itself (pitch detection). Dynamics become velocity layers, takes become round robins, and a pack with several articulations keeps all of them, selected by <text:span text:style-name="T2">keyswitches</text:span> on the keys below the playing range. Each instrument is levelled on loading, so a flute and a tuba start at the same height, and large libraries stream from disk instead of loading whole into memory.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T2">The panel.</text:span> A tab with the sampler shows its own panel in the RACK:</text:p>
   <text:list text:style-name="L2">
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="T2">LOAD</text:span> (with the folder it is playing) and <text:span text:style-name="T2">CLEAR</text:span>.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14">The layout: <text:span text:style-name="T2">AUTO</text:span> (worked out from the files), <text:span text:style-name="T2">STRETCH</text:span> (one sample across the keyboard), <text:span text:style-name="T2">KIT</text:span> (one sample a key, drums on their GM keys) and <text:span text:style-name="T2">SLICE</text:span>, which cuts the longest file into 8, 16, 24 or 32 pieces, one a key from C2.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14">▶ / ■ to audition without a keyboard, with the key (<text:span text:style-name="T2">NOTE</text:span>) and the velocity (<text:span text:style-name="T2">V</text:span>) it plays, and the articulation when the pack has several.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="T2">MAP</text:span> opens the mapping editor: a row a region with root, low key, high key and tune. Arrows move, PageUp/PageDown move by an octave, F2 saves an <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">.smpreset</text:span> beside the folder and reloads the tab from it, F3 relinks a library that moved, Esc discards.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P15">The waveform of the sample the key plays, with the start point and the slices marked — a click on it sets the start — and the envelope, which can be dragged.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
   </text:list>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">Its knobs are ordinary instrument parameters — <text:span text:style-name="T2">START</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">LOOP</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">ATTACK</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">DECAY</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">SUSTAIN</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">RELEASE</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">LOOP ST</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">LOOP END</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="T2">XFADE</text:span> — so they are saved with the project, move with the arrows and can be MIDI-learned. The loop runs while the key is down and is crossfaded at the join. Channel and polyphonic aftertouch arrive as expression (CC 11).</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">Recording a sampler. REC on the panel records the tab&apos;s audio input — before the trim, up to 60 seconds; on a tab with no input it does nothing and says so in the log. Press it again to stop: the take is cut at its silences, one WAV per sound, and loaded into the sampler at once. The files carry no note in their names, so the sampler reads each one&apos;s pitch off the audio, spreads the pitched ones over the keyboard and builds a kit from the rest; two takes of the same note become round robins. Pair it with A→M and FOLLOW and anything you record can be played by your voice or your guitar.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">SAVE, or it goes. A new take is kept in a scratch folder and SAVE appears on the panel: it moves the take to ~/.local/state/choz/recordings (a sample path of its own, so SOURCE lists every saved take and a project finds it again) and reloads the sampler from there. A take nobody saved is deleted as soon as nothing plays it — another instrument, CLEAR, a project loaded or the tab closed — and whatever is left when choz exits. Save a take before saving a project that plays it.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_2" text:outline-level="2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2451_3455341890"/>6. Effects<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2451_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2453_3455341890"/>6.1 The catalogue<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2453_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">a</text:span> (or <text:span text:style-name="T2">[+ ADD]</text:span> in the FX CHAIN section) opens it. The left column is categories with how many effects each holds; the right column is the list. The tabs filter by format, and <text:span text:style-name="T2">BUILT-IN</text:span> leaves only choz&apos;s own.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr1" draw:name="Imagen12" text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:width="15.499cm" svg:height="8.4cm"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png">
