   <text:p text:style-name="Cuerpo_20_de_20_texto.cap">Figure 6.2 — The internal reverb in the chain, with its own parameters.</text:p>
   <table:table table:name="Tabla9" table:style-name="Tabla9">
    <table:table-column table:style-name="Tabla9.A"/>
    <table:table-column table:style-name="Tabla9.B"/>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla9.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Heading">Key</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla9.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Heading">Action</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla9.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Space</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla9.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Switch the selected effect on or off</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla9.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">← →</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla9.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Move between effects in the chain</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla9.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">↑ ↓, wheel, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">w</text:span>/<text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">s</text:span></text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla9.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Pick a knob and move it</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla9.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents"><text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">d</text:span></text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla9.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Delete the selected effect</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla9.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents"><text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">G</text:span></text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla9.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Open the effect&apos;s own window, if it is a plugin that has one</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla9.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents"><text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">X</text:span></text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla9.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Run the effect isolated in another process</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
   </table:table>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2457_3455341890"/>6.3 The built-in effects<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2457_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">Fifty-six effects ship with choz. They need no plugin, they are always present, and they are the ones published in <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">choz.clap</text:span> for other hosts.</text:p>
   <table:table table:name="Tabla10" table:style-name="Tabla10">
    <table:table-column table:style-name="Tabla10.A"/>
    <table:table-column table:style-name="Tabla10.B"/>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Heading">Category</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Heading">Effects</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Delay (5)</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">DELAY, GRANDELAY, SPACE ECHO, REVERSE, MULTI-TAP</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Reverb (3)</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">REVERB, SHIMMER, PLATE</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Dynamics (9)</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">COMPRESSOR, LIMITER, GATE, EXPANDER, SIDECHAIN, DE-ESSER, TRANSIENT, MULTIBAND, ENVELOPE</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">EQ / Filter (6)</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">PARAM EQ, GRAPHIC EQ, FILTER, FILTERBANK, ISOLATOR, LADDER</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Modulation (9)</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">CHORUS, FLANGER, PHASER, TREMOLO, AUTO PAN, AUTO FILTER, FREQ SHIFT, RING MOD, VIBRATO</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Distortion (11)</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">BITCRUSH, VINYL, CASSETTE, SOFTCLIP, SATURATOR, WAVESHAPER, TUBE SAT, AMBER FANG, VELVET FUZZ, EXCITER, BASS ENH</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Spatial (2)</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">WIDENER, PAN</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Texture (4)</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">BEAT REPEAT, LOOPER, PROTOCOSMOS, Z5 TEXTURE</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Pitch (4)</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">HARMONIZER, VOCODER, AUTO-TUNE, PITCH SHIFT</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">Utility (3)</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Tabla10.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents">GAIN, PHASE INV, MONO</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
   </table:table>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Tape, clouds and textures.</text:span> SPACE ECHO picks its heads like the machine it is modelled on — <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Heads</text:span>: 1, 2, 3, 1+2, 2+3, 1+3, 1+2+3 or REV, the spring alone — with the heads evenly spaced 1:2:3, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Bass</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Treble</text:span> on the echoes, and a spring that hears the input as well as the repeats. PROTOCOSMOS has <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Repeats</text:span> (its feedback) and a resonant low-pass <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Filter</text:span> on the cloud. Both follow the tempo with <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Sync</text:span> (FREE, or 1/32 to a whole bar): SPACE ECHO puts its heads on the grid, PROTOCOSMOS starts one grain per division. Z5 TEXTURE has <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Contour</text:span>, the shape of its grains (percussive to flat-topped, the middle is the classic window), and an LFO of its own (<text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Rate</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Depth</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Shape</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Dest</text:span>). Every one of these starts where the effect sounded before, so an older project opens unchanged.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T2">The looper.</text:span> Each channel strip has its own REC, PLAY (which is also PAUSE) and STOP; the row under the strips works the whole deck. <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">▶ ALL</text:span> starts every channel that holds a take, and while anything is playing it reads <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">▌▌ ALL</text:span> and pauses them all in place (a channel being recorded is closed and keeps playing). <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">■ ALL</text:span> stops every channel. Channel 1 sets the loop&apos;s length; REC on another channel before that length exists does nothing, and pressing it again once channel 1 is closed starts the take.</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2459_3455341890"/>6.4 Effect presets<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2459_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">Each slot can save and load a preset of its own effect, so a compressor setting that works on a voice can be carried to another tab or another project without saving the whole rack.</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2461_3455341890"/>6.5 Chord source and the gate<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2461_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:list text:style-name="L8">
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P27"><text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">C</text:span> chooses which keyboard the selected effect takes its chord from — the harmoniser, for instance, with two controllers on a hub. </text:p>
     <text:p text:style-name="P27"><text:span text:style-name="T2">How the HARMONIZER sings.</text:span> It listens for the note being sung (the same detector as AUTO-TUNE, with a 32 ms window: a new note is followed in under 40 ms) and builds every voice from it: the note, plus the interval the <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Shape</text:span> names — <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">3rds</text:span> a third and a fifth, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">OCT</text:span> octaves, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">CHORD</text:span> any chord the arranger can write — moved onto the nearest note of the <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Key</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Scale</text:span>. A third over C in C major is E; over D it is F. <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Chromatic</text:span> keeps the intervals as written, which is the parallel sound. With a chord to follow, the voices land on that chord&apos;s notes instead of the scale&apos;s; with nothing sung yet, the chord itself is the harmony. A note counts once it has held for a few milliseconds, so the first instant of an attack does not move the voices; after a second of silence the note is forgotten and the next one is measured from itself. No two voices sing the same note, and they glide to a new harmony rather than jumping. <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Env</text:span> closes the voices only in real gaps: a decaying note keeps its harmony. <text:span text:style-name="T2">MIDI on the keys that play the tab</text:span>: with no keyboard picked in <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">CHORD</text:span>, the chord followed is the notes the instrument is already playing, and the voices double them — the log says so once. Pick another keyboard, or switch <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">MIDI</text:span> off to harmonise on the scale or a chart.</text:p>
     <text:p text:style-name="P27"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Voices that sound like voices.</text:span> Each voice is moved with PSOLA: short grains cut at every pulse of the voice and laid down again at the new pitch, so the vowels stay where the mouth put them and a voice two octaves away is still a voice, not the singer in a tube. With a chord to follow, the voices move like a choir — each one to the chord tone nearest where it was, keeping common notes — instead of all jumping with the melody. <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">CHORD</text:span> in <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Shape</text:span> opens the arranger&apos;s chord dialogue (FAMILY, 6/7, TENSION, 9, 11, 13, 5; ←→ turn a row) without a root: the root is the note being sung. <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">OCT</text:span> on the GATE/CHORD row opens the piano to choose the octave the voices sing in — click a key, ←→ to step, A for AUTO — and no voice goes further than two octaves from the singer. <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Lead</text:span> is how much of the singer comes out with the harmony: at its default, 0, a fully wet harmoniser gives only the voices, which is what a call through choz Mic should hear. <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">ARR</text:span> on the chart row follows the arranger instead: while any tab&apos;s arranger plays, its chord is the harmony.</text:p>
     <text:p text:style-name="P27">Shapes, places and octaves. Voices is a slider from 1 to 10. SHAPE is listed by category: STACKS (3rds, 5ths, OCT, 4THS — a chord in fourths), INTERVALS (2m to 7M) and CHORD. Place decides where the voices of every shape but CHORD go: ABOVE, BELOW, MIDDLE (the default, alternating up and down) or CLUSTER (the same notes folded to within a tritone of the sung note). A new harmoniser opens chromatic, with no key. RESET on the GATE/CHORD row puts every knob back to how a new one opens. OCT cycles ON → OFF → AUTO: ON opens the piano to choose the octave (CANCEL goes back), OFF leaves the voices where the shape puts them, AUTO keeps every voice within an octave of the singer. The CHORD dialogue&apos;s rows are no longer on the knob grid, but they are still parameters: saved and learnable. The mouse wheel works in the harmoniser&apos;s dialogues: in a list it moves one row and applies it without closing, so you can try shapes by ear.</text:p>
     <text:p text:style-name="P27"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Following a chart.</text:span> The row under GATE and CHORD plays a <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">.chord</text:span> progression for the harmoniser to follow, the way the arranger plays one for its band: <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">LOAD</text:span> picks the file (<text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">.chord</text:span> or <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">.mid</text:span>, kept in the project), <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">▶</text:span> starts it on the next downbeat of the metronome and switches the effect&apos;s <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Chart</text:span> on, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">■</text:span> stops it and puts the harmony back on its scale (or on the keyboard, with <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">MIDI</text:span> on). The lights are the metronome&apos;s beats; while the chart plays, the one lit is the beat the click is on (stopped, they stand still) — the same count the beep follows, bar by bar through a chart that changes meter — and beside them the chord and the bar the chart is at. <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">CLICK</text:span> is the metronome&apos;s own switch. From the keyboard: <text:span text:style-name="T2">y</text:span> is ▶/■, <text:span text:style-name="T2">Y</text:span> is LOAD and <text:span text:style-name="T2">o</text:span> is CLICK. One harmoniser follows a chart at a time; the chart takes precedence over a keyboard chord while it plays. The log (<text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">~/.local/state/choz/choz.log</text:span>) says what the harmoniser did: ▶ and ■, every chord the chart moves to, and once a second while anything changes the note it hears, how often its voices moved and where to, and its output peak.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P28"><text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">c</text:span> opens the gate: another tab drives this effect&apos;s dry/wet or one of its knobs, with its level or with the notes it receives. That is how a kick ducks a pad, or a vocal opens a delay. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
   </text:list>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_2" text:outline-level="2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2463_3455341890"/>7. Plugins<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2463_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2465_3455341890"/>7.1 Formats<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2465_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">choz hosts CLAP, LV2, VST2, VST3, LADSPA, DSSI and Pure Data patches, as instruments and as effects. What appears in the rack is what the plugin declares: a parameter that says it cannot be automated is not drawn as a knob, and a parameter whose whole integer range reads as two words is drawn as a switch rather than a fader.</text:p>
