   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_2" text:outline-level="2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2489_3455341890"/>9. Arpeggiator, step sequencer and arranger<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2489_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2491_3455341890"/>9.1 Arpeggiator<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2491_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">A</text:span> switches on the active tab&apos;s arpeggiator (so does the <text:span text:style-name="T2">ARP</text:span> button). Its controls are the mode (UP, DOWN and the rest), the division (1/16, 1/8 …), BPM, gate, swing, octaves, <text:span text:style-name="T2">HOLD</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">CHORD</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="T2">TAP</text:span>. HOLD behaves like a hardware sequencer&apos;s: letting go keeps the chord sounding, and the next key pressed with nothing held starts a new chord instead of adding to the old one.</text:p>
