   <text:p text:style-name="Cuerpo_20_de_20_texto.cap">Figure 9.1 — The tab&apos;s arpeggiator: mode, division, BPM, gate, swing, octaves, hold, chord and tap.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T2">One clock.</text:span> The arpeggiator, the sequencer, the arranger and the metronome count the same position — the transport while it rolls, a free clock that never stops otherwise — so anything started at any time lands on the same grid. The first note of a chord sounds when the key is pressed; the rest fall on the grid. Swing lengthens the same steps it does in the sequencer.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2493_3455341890"/>9.2 Step sequencer<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2493_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">Every tab also carries a sequencer built like a hardware step machine: eight tracks (A–H), sixteen steps, eight parts and a song chain. It is drawn above the instrument because that is the order the notes travel in. <text:span text:style-name="T2">REC</text:span> writes what you play, quantised to the step the play head is on, and each step passes through the tab&apos;s arpeggiator when there is one.</text:p>
