   <text:p text:style-name="Cuerpo_20_de_20_texto.cap">Figure 9.3 — The arranger on a SoundFont, playing the heterometric Tarkus chart: the band row with SPLIT OUT, the bar the band counts (5/4) and the chart, bar 5 lit.</text:p>
   <text:list text:style-name="L2">
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="T2">▶</text:span> plays the form from bar one, <text:span text:style-name="T2">⏸</text:span> leaves and comes back at the top of the bar it was left in, <text:span text:style-name="T2">■</text:span> stops, lets go of everything and rewinds (and takes the click, as in the sequencer).</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="T2">▶ comes in on the next downbeat</text:span> of the metronome&apos;s bar, not on the press, so the band&apos;s one is the click&apos;s one — at most a bar&apos;s wait, half a bar on average. Pressed on the one (up to an eighth of a beat late) it starts that bar at once.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="T2">KEY</text:span> picks the tonic on a piano; the style button opens the list of every style (about two hundred, measured from real accompaniments), each described by how it plays — and <text:span text:style-name="T2">choosing one loads its time signature</text:span> (a waltz 3/4, a 6/8 blues 6/8) into METER, the metronome and the session, unless the chart names its own; <text:span text:style-name="T2">SEED</text:span> picks another interpretation of the same chart.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="T2">C–F–G / I–IV–V</text:span> switches the chart between chord letters and roman degrees — it rewrites the text.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="T2">BASS DRUMS PIANO GUITAR</text:span>: a click puts a musician in or out of the band, the wheel over the bar sets how loud. On a SoundFont every musician gets its own program (a kit for the drums, a bass for the bass); on other instruments the whole band plays the tab&apos;s one sound. The <text:span text:style-name="T2">right button</text:span> on a musician opens the SoundFont&apos;s programs for that musician alone (BANK/PRESET), with AUTO on top — the style&apos;s choice, named beside it; the pick is kept in the project. <text:span text:style-name="T2">n</text:span> in the MIXER on a tab playing a band levels it musician by musician, from the loudest each one played since the last time, and puts the tab back at 1.00: a font&apos;s kit can sit 15 dB over its piano, which the faders here — they set how hard each one plays — cannot make up. Loading an instrument only ever turns its tab down, never up: one middle C is not a band.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="T2">SPLIT OUT</text:span> (off by default) puts every musician on a strip of their own in the MIXER — after the tabs, before the groups — without opening any tab: a fader, a pan and a mute on that musician&apos;s channel of the tab&apos;s instrument, with its own meter. It needs the band to have channels to separate, which a SoundFont with the programs for it gives; reverb and chorus stay on the tab.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="T2">METER</text:span> is the bar the band counts; <text:span text:style-name="T2">SWING</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">RAND</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="T2">PROB</text:span> vary the part the way the sequencer&apos;s knobs do.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="T2">LOAD</text:span> opens a <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">.chord</text:span> chart or a MIDI file (section 9.5); <text:span text:style-name="T2">EXPORT MIDI</text:span> writes the band out (section 9.5).</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P15">The bars at the bottom of the box are the progression: a click opens the grid editor, a chord a subdivision, with <text:span text:style-name="T2">CHORD</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">GROUP</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">HOLD</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">BAR +</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="T2">BAR -</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="T2">BAND BAR</text:span>. Every bar is drawn once, however often the form plays it: <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">form = turn turn turn</text:span> is the twelve bars of the turn, and the light goes back to its first bar each time round, with the header saying which pass it is (<text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">BAR 13 [TURN] 2/3</text:span>). Every chord of a bar is played by every musician: a chord the style&apos;s pattern has no hit for is struck where it starts.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
   </text:list>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___TocChoz4_2026"/>9.4 Chord charts<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___TocChoz4_2026"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">A chart is a plain-text <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">.chord</text:span> file. The settings go on top, the bars below; <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">#</text:span> starts a comment. A fresh tab plays a twelve-bar blues in C — in the source tree it is <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">assets/default.chord</text:span>, a chart like any other.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">key = D</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">style = strtrock</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">meter = 7/8 # or: time = 7/8</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">groups = 3+2+2 # or: grouping = 3 2 2</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">[A]</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">| Dm7 | G7:3 C:4 | Cmaj7 - | A7 |</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">[B]</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">| IIm7 | V7 | I | % |</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Preformatted_20_Text">form = A A B A</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:list text:style-name="L2">
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">|</text:span> separates bars, and so does the end of a line. A bar with two chords splits it evenly; <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">C:3</text:span> gives a chord three of the bar&apos;s units.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="T2">A chart can change meter</text:span>: a bar that starts with a signature is in it, and so is every bar after it in the part until the next one — <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">| 5/4 Fm | Eb | 3/4 Db | 7/8 Cm |</text:span>. Each bar lasts what its signature says; a part starts back in the chart&apos;s bar. The source tree has an example, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">assets/tarkus.chord</text:span>, for the <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">tarkus</text:span> style — which has a groove for each of the signatures it changes to.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">-</text:span> holds the chord before it, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">%</text:span> repeats the bar before.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14">Letters (<text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">C7</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">F#m7b5</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">Bbmaj9</text:span>) or degrees against the key (<text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">I7</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">IVm7</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">VIIb7</text:span>).</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">[A]</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">[intro]</text:span>: parts; <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">form</text:span> says which are played in what order (without it, the order they were written in).</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P14"><text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">meter</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">groups</text:span>: the bar the chart is written in and how it is counted. They win over the session and the style, and <text:span text:style-name="T2">LOAD</text:span> hands them to the metronome and the grid. Groups that do not add up to the numerator are an error. A chart that changes meter counts each meter with a line of its own — <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">groups 7/8 = 2+2+3</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">groups 5/8 = 3+2</text:span> — and while the band plays, the click accents the grouping of the bar it is in; a meter with no line gets the downbeat alone.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P15">A chart that does not read leaves the band playing what it had, and says why on the box.</text:p>
    </text:list-item>
   </text:list>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Styles.</text:span> <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">style =</text:span> takes a style by its id, its name or the label the picker shows — <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">style = 42</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">style = drumnbass</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">style = Drum &amp; Bass</text:span> are the same chart. The id is the number the STYLE list shows beside each style, and it does not change when styles are added. A name that matches none plays the first style. Every style is measured off a rhythm; the definition says what the band plays in it.</text:p>
   <table:table table:name="TablaStyles" table:style-name="TablaStyles">
