   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___TocChoz5_2026"/>9.5 MIDI import and export<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___TocChoz5_2026"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T2">EXPORT MIDI</text:span> asks for a folder and then a file name (<text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">.mid</text:span> is added; an existing file is asked about). The file is type 1: a conductor track with the tempo, the time signature, a marker at every chord change and the chart itself, then one track per musician on the channel a GM player expects — drums on 10 — with the style&apos;s program.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Levels.</text:span> Inside a tab the whole band plays one instrument, so the parts are baked to sit under each other. In the file each musician has a channel of its own, so each part is brought to its own average velocity — drums 96, bass 92, piano 80, guitar 74, accents kept — and each musician&apos;s fader goes on the channel volume (CC 7; 100 at full), where the DAW&apos;s mixer can find it.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T2">LOAD</text:span> opens <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">.chord</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">.mid</text:span> and <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">.midi</text:span>. A MIDI file choz wrote opens as exactly the chart that wrote it. Any other is listened to: one chord a bar, or two when the halves of the bar are plainly different, with the root taken from the bass register and the drum channel ignored. It is a heuristic — a melody full of passing notes can split a bar that is one chord — and the grid editor is where to correct it.</text:p>
   <table:table table:name="TablaChoz103" table:style-name="TablaChoz103">
    <table:table-column table:style-name="TablaChoz103.A"/>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="TablaChoz103.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="Table_20_Contents"><text:span text:style-name="T2">Note.</text:span> The arranger reads and writes <text:span text:style-name="T2">two to four instruments</text:span> (channels with notes, drums included). A single melody or a full GM song is not a backing and is refused; so is exporting a band of one — put a second musician in first.</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
   </table:table>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___TocChoz6_2026"/>9.6 The arranger and the click<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___TocChoz6_2026"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">While an arranger plays, the metronome <text:span text:style-name="T2">follows its bar</text:span>: the chart&apos;s <text:span text:style-name="Source_20_Text">meter</text:span>, else the style&apos;s own (a waltz clicks in three), with its grouping. The sequencer&apos;s grid follows too, and the session&apos;s own time signature comes back when the band stops. In a chart that changes meter the click changes with every bar, and counts its one from that bar&apos;s downbeat. <text:span text:style-name="T2">FOLLOW ARR</text:span> in the metronome&apos;s menu turns this off; setting BEATS or UNIT by hand turns it off as well, because that is the player taking the count back.</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_2" text:outline-level="2"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2495_3455341890"/>10. Transport, tempo and metronome<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2495_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2497_3455341890"/>10.1 Transport<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2497_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T2">F7</text:span> starts and stops the rack, <text:span text:style-name="T2">F8</text:span> arms automation recording, <text:span text:style-name="T2">F9</text:span> fires panic — the same three controls that sit on the menu bar. The transport is what every sequencer, every synced delay and the automation count against.</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_3" text:outline-level="3"><text:bookmark-start text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2499_3455341890"/>10.2 Metronome<text:bookmark-end text:name="__RefHeading___Toc2499_3455341890"/></text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><text:span text:style-name="T2">F6</text:span> switches the click on; the ▾ beside <text:span text:style-name="T2">MET</text:span> opens its options: click, tempo, beats and unit of the bar, grouping, sound, output and level. It keeps counting with the transport stopped — which is usually when it is needed — and it counts the same tempo the tempo-synced plugins read.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr1" draw:name="Imagen27" text:anchor-type="as-char" svg:width="15.499cm" svg:height="8.4cm"><draw:image draw:mime-type="image/png">
